tion**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{P}_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that have to be covered.  \nFor a line \\(\\ell\\) write  \n\n* \\(\\ell\\) is **vertical** if it is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, i.e. \\(\\ell:x=i\\) for some integer \\(i\\);\n* \\(\\ell\\) is **horizontal** if it is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, i.e. \\(\\ell:y=j\\) for some integer \\(j\\);\n* \\(\\ell\\) is **diagonal** if it is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\), i.e. \\(\\ell:x+y=s\\) for some integer \\(s\\).\n\nA line is **sunny** iff it is none of the three types above.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Geometry of the three families\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\;(1\\le i\\le n)\\) the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly the points  \n\n\\[\n\\{(i,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, for each \\(j\\;(1\\le j\\le n)\\) the horizontal line  \n\n\\[\nH_j:\\;y=j\n\\]\n\ncontains the points  \n\n\\[\n\\{(a,j)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n+1-j\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nFinally, for each integer \\(s\\;(2\\le s\\le n+1)\\) the diagonal line  \n\n\\[\nD_s:\\;x+y=s\n\\]\n\ncontains the points  \n\n\\[\n\\{(a,s-a)\\mid 1\\le a\\le s-1\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nEvery point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) belongs to exactly one line of each of the three families (1)–(3).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A counting lemma\n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma.}\\qquad\n\\text{Let }i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}. \n\\text{If the vertical line }V_i\\text{ is **not** among the }n\\text{ covering lines,}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\text{then at least }n+1-i\\text{ sunny lines are needed to cover the points of column }i,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\text{and each such sunny line meets any other column in at most one point.}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\(V_i\\) is absent, the points \\((i,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\) must be covered by other lines.  \nA sunny line is not vertical, 