vention that for \\(t=1\\) we take the line through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((n,n)\\), whose slope is \\(1\\)).  This slope is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); hence each \\(L_t\\) is sunny.  A direct verification shows that every \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on the line \\(L_a\\); consequently the family \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n3. **Existence of other values of \\(k\\).**  \n   By explicit enumeration for the smallest non‑trivial case \\(n=3\\) one obtains a covering with exactly one sunny line:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &\\text{vertical line }x=1,\\\\\n   &\\text{horizontal line }y=1,\\\\\n   &\\text{sunny line }y=x\\;(\\text{slope }1).\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n   These three distinct lines cover the points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\) of \\(\\mathcal P_3\\).  Hence \\(k=1\\) is admissible when \\(n=3\\).\n\n4. **Partial conclusion.**  \n   The constructions above prove that \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n\\) are always possible, and the example for \\(n=3\\) shows that other intermediate values of \\(k\\) can occur.  Determining the complete set of admissible \\(k\\) for a general \\(n\\) remains an open problem.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
