fying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved that for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-2\\}} .\n\\]\n\nIn other words, a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition exists **iff** the number of sunny lines is at most \\(n-2\\); every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) can be realised.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Upper bound \\(k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   * The \\(n\\) points on the diagonal  \n     \\[\n     D=\\{(i,n+1-i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}\n     \\]  \n     are pairwise joined by the line of slope \\(-1\\); any line of a different slope (i.e. a sunny line) can contain **at most one** point of \\(D\\).  \n   * Consequently at least one non‑sunny line is needed to cover more than one point of \\(D\\); the only non‑sunny line that can contain two points of \\(D\\) is the diagonal itself, so the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) must belong to every admissible configuration.  \n   * The points \\((1,b)\\;(1\\le b\\le n-1)\\) lie on the first vertical line \\(x=1\\) and are pairwise distinct; a sunny line cannot contain two of them (otherwise it would be vertical). Hence a second non‑sunny line, namely the vertical line \\(x=1\\), is also unavoidable.  \n   * Thus any admissible family of \\(n\\) lines contains **at least two** non‑sunny lines, whence  \n     \\[\n     k\\le n-2 .\n     \\]\n\n2. **Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   * Fix the two mandatory non‑sunny lines:\n     \\[\n     L_{v}:x=1 ,\\qquad L_{d}:x+y=n+1 .\n     \\]\n   * For each integer \\(i\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le n-1\\) define the *diagonal‑through‑\\((1,1)\\) line*  \n     \\[\n     S_{i}:\\; \\text{the line through }(1,1)\\text{ and }(i,n+1-i).\n     \