y of \\(n\\) lines containing exactly \\(n-2\\) sunny ones.  \n\n   *\\(n=3\\).*  A direct inspection shows that the four values \\(k=0,1,2,3\\) are attainable.\n\n   *\\(k=n-1\\) for \\(n\\ge 4\\) is impossible.  \n     Indeed, each sunny line can contain at most one diagonal point and at most one further point of \\(S_n\\); therefore the \\(k\\) sunny lines cover at most \\(2k\\) points. The remaining \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines can cover at most \\(n+(n-k-1)\\) points (the diagonal and at most \\(n-k-1\\) vertical lines). Hence the total number of points that can be covered is at most  \n\n     \\[\n     2k+n+(n-k-1)=3k+n-1 .\n     \\]\n\n     Requiring \\(3k+n-1\\ge \\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) gives \\(k\\le n-2\\) for every \\(n\\ge 4\\). Hence \\(k=n-1\\) is impossible when \\(n\\ge 4\\).\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\begin{cases}\n   \\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n   \\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}, & n\\ge 4 .\n   \\end{cases}}\n\\]   This list is exhaustive and each listed value is realised by the constructions described above.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
