t from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A necessary lower bound for the number of non‑sunny lines\n\nConsider the three subsets of \\(S_n\\)\n\n\\[\nV=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\},\\qquad\nH=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\},\\qquad\nD=\\{(a,n+1-a)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(V\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=1\\), \\(H\\) on the horizontal line\n\\(y=1\\) and \\(D\\) on the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n\nA sunny line can intersect each of the three sets in at most one point,\nbecause two distinct points of any of the three sets determine a line\nparallel respectively to the \\(y\\)-axis, the \\(x\\)-axis or the line\n\\(x+y=0\\).\n\nHence, in order to cover all points of \\(V\\) we need either the line\n\\(x=1\\) or at least \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines; similarly for \\(H\\) and\n\\(D\\).  Since we are allowed only \\(n\\) lines in total, the three\nnon‑sunny lines\n\n\\[\nL_V:x=1,\\qquad L_H:y=1,\\qquad L_D:x+y=n+1\n\\]\n\nmust all be present.  Consequently at least three of the \\(n\\) lines are\nnon‑sunny, i.e.\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-3\\qquad (n\\ge 3). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction attaining every admissible value of \\(k\\)\n\nFor an integer \\(t\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le n-3\\) we construct a family\n\\(\\mathcal L_t\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that contains exactly \\(t\\) sunny\nlines.\n\n*The three mandatory non‑sunny lines*  \n\n\\[\nL_V:x=1,\\qquad L_H:y=1,\\qquad L_D:x+y=n+1\n\\]\n\nare always included.\n\n*The remaining \\(n-3\\) lines* are chosen as follows.\n\n*Vertical lines.*  \nFor each integer \\(i\\) with  \n\n\\[\n2\\le i\\le n-t-1\n\\]\n\ntake the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i .\n\\]\n\nThere are \\(n-t-2\\) such lines; each of them is non‑sunny.\n\n*Sunny lines of slope \\(1\\).*  \nFor each integer \\(j\\) with  \n\n\\[\n1\\le j\\le t\n\\]\n\ntake the line  \n\n\\[\nS_j:\\; y = x + (j-1) .\n\\]\n\nIts slope is \\(1\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore each \\(S_j\\) is sunny.\nThere are exactly \\(t\\) of them.\n\nThe total number of lines is  \n\n\\[\n3\\;(\\text{border})\\;+\\;(n-t-2)\\;(\\text{vertical})\\;+\\;t\\;(\\text{sunny})\n= n .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  Verification that all points of \\(S_n\\) are covered\n\n*Border points.*  \nAll points of \\(V\\), \\(H\\) and \\(D\\) lie on \\(L_V\\), \\(L_H\\) and \\(L_D\\)\nrespectively.\n\n*Points with \\(y=1\\) (the bottom side).*  \nThey are on the horizontal line \\(L_H\\).\n\n*Points with \\(x=1\\) (the left side).*  \nThey are on the vertical line \\(L_V\\).\n\n*Points with \\(x+y=n+1\\) (the hypotenuse).*  \nThey are on the diagonal line \\(L_D\\).\n\nIt remains to consider the interior points  \n\n\\[\nI_n=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2,\\;a+b\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in I_n\\).  Two cases are possible.\n\n1. **\\(a\\le n-t-1\\).**  \n   Then the point lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\), which belongs to\n   \\(\\mathcal L_t\\).\n\n2. **\\(a\\ge n-t\\).**  \n   From \\(a+b\\le n\\) we obtain \\(b\\le t+1\\).  Hence\n   \\(d:=b-a\\) satisfies \\(- (t-1)\\le d\\le 0\\), i.e. \\(d=j-1\\) for some\n   \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,t\\}\\).  Consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line\n   \\(S_j\\) (because \\(b = a + (j-1)\\)).\n\nThus every interior point belongs either to one of the vertical lines\n\\(V_i\\) or to one of the sunny lines \\(S_j\\).  All points of \\(S_n\\) are\ncovered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Determination of the admissible values of \\(k\\)\n\nThe construction above yields, for each integer \\(t\\) with\n\\(0\\le t\\le n-3\\), a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers\n\\(S_n\\) and contains exactly \\(t\\) sunny lines.  Hence every integer\n\\(k\\) in the interval \\([0,n-3]\\) is attainable.\n\nTogether with the necessary condition (1) we obtain the