tersect \\(D_5\\) in distinct points. The argument does not rule out configurations where one non‑sunny line is the diagonal and the remaining three lines are sunny, which would give \\(k=3\\) (already known) but does not preclude the possibility of exactly two sunny lines with a different arrangement. A rigorous impossibility proof would need to consider all ways two sunny and two non‑sunny lines could cover \\(D_5\\) and the rest of \\(S_4\\).  \n  Hence there is a **Justification Gap** in this part of the proof.\n\n### Section 4 – General case \\(n\\ge5\\)\n\n- The solution sets \\(s=k\\) sunny lines and \\(t=n-k\\) non‑sunny diagonals (4).  \n- It claims the non‑sunny diagonals (the largest \\(t\\) diagonals) cover the points on those diagonals, leaving a smaller triangular set of size \\(n-t=k\\).  \n- Then it re‑uses the construction (5):  \n  \\[\n  L_j:\\; y=\\frac{j-1}{j}x+1\\qquad(j=1,\\dots ,s).\n  \\]\n- As noted earlier, for \\(j=1\\) this line is \\(y=1\\), a horizontal (non‑sunny) line. Consequently the family \\(\\{L_j\\}\\) contains exactly \\(s-1\\) sunny lines, not \\(s\\).  \n- Therefore the claimed configuration has only \\(k-1\\) sunny lines, contradicting the requirement of exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n- Because the construction fails for every \\(k\\ge1\\), the statement “for every \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) a required configuration exists” is unsupported.  \n- **Critical Error:** the general construction does not produce the intended number of sunny lines.\n\n### Section 5 – Conclusion\n\n- The conclusion aggregates the (incorrect) results from Sections 3 and 4. Since the proofs for \\(k=n\\) (all sunny) and for arbitrary \\(k\\) when \\(n\\ge5\\) are invalid, the final table is not justified.\n\n---\n\n**Overall Assessment:** The solution contains critical logical mistakes in the core constructions and an unjustified impossibility argument for one sub‑case. Consequently the answer does not correctly solve the problem."
