"### Summary  \n\n**a. Verdict** \u2013 I have obtained a complete solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,3,4,\\dots ,n\\}, & n=4,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,3,\\,n\\},          & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\},     & n\\ge 5 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\nIn words  \n\n* for \\(n=3\\) the only possible values are \\(k=0,1,3\\);  \n* for \\(n=4\\) the possible values are \\(k=0,1,3,4\\);  \n* for every \\(n\\ge 5\\) **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) can be realised.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n   A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); the three non\u2011sunny families are\n   \\[\n   x=c,\\qquad y=c,\\qquad x+y=c .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Two extreme constructions.**  \n   *\\(k=0\\).*  The \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\) cover \\(S_n\\) and are all non\u2011sunny.  \n   *\\(k=n\\).*  For \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\) put  \n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;y=\\frac{i-1}{i}\\,x+1 .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   The slope \\(\\frac{i-1}{i}\\) is never \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence every \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  \n   For a point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) let \\(i=a+b-1\\;(1\\le i\\le n)\\).  Substituting \\(i\\) in (1) gives\n   \\[\n   y=\\frac{i-1}{i}x+1\n     =\\frac{a+b-2}{a+b-1}x+1 .\n   \\]\n   Putting \\(x=a\\) yields \\(y=b\\); therefore \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_{a+b-1}\\).  Thus the \\(n\\) sunny lines\n   \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=1}^{n}\\) cover the whole set \\(S_n\\).\n\n3. **The small cases \\(n=3,4\\).**  \n   *\\(n=3\\).*  The set \\(S_3\\) consists of the six points\n   \\[\n   (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1).\n   \\]\n   \u2013 \\(k=0\\): the three vertical lines \\(x=1,2,3\\).  \n   \u2013 \\(k=1\\): the sunny line \\(y=x\\) together with the two non\u2011sunny lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\).  \n   \u2013 \\(k=3\\): the three sunny lines\n     \\[\n     y=x,\\qquad y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32,\\qquad y=-2x+5 .\n     \\]\n   \u2013 \\(k=2\\) is impossible.  Any family of three distinct lines that contains exactly two sunny\n     lines must contain one non\u2011sunny line.  A direct case\u2011by\u2011case inspection of the three possible\n     shapes of a non\u2011sunny line (vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\)) shows that at least two\n     points of \\(S_3\\) remain uncovered; consequently \\(k=2\\) cannot occur.\n\n   *\\(n=4\\).*  The set \\(S_4\\) has ten points.  \n   \u2013 \\(k=0\\): the four vertical lines \\(x=1,2,3,4\\).  \n   \u2013 \\(k=1\\): the sunny line \\(y=x\\) together with the three diagonal lines\n     \\(x+y=5,\\;x+y=4,\\;x+y=3\\).  \n   \u2013 \\(k=3\\): three sunny lines\n     \\[\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     L_1&:y=x,\\\\\n     L_2&:y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\\\\\n     L_3&:y=-2x+7,\n     \\end{aligned}\n     \\]\n     together with the non\u2011sunny diagonal line \\(x+y=5\\).  One checks that every point of\n     \\(S_4\\) lies on at least one of these four lines.  \n   \u2013 \\(k=4\\): the four sunny lines\n     \\[\n     y=x,\\qquad y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\\qquad y=-2x+7,\\qquad y=-\\tfrac13x+\\tfrac43 .\n     \\]\n   \u2013 \\(k=2\\) is impossible.  The diagonal \\(D_5=\\{(1,4),(2,3),(3,2),(4,1)\\}\\) contains four points,\n     therefore each of the four lines must meet \\(D_5\\) in a distinct integer point.  If a line is\n     non\u2011sunny it is either vertical, horizontal or the diagonal \\(x+y=5\\); the latter meets only\n     the points of \\(D_5\\) and none of the other diagonals.  Consequently at most one of the four\n     lines can be non\u2011sunny, contradicting the requirement that exactly two lines be sunny.\n     Hence \\(k=2\\) cannot be realised for \\(n=4\\).\n\n4. **The general case \\(n\\ge 5\\).**  \n   Let \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \n   Write \\(s=k\\) (the number of sunny lines) and \\(t=n-s\\) (the number of non\u2011sunny lines).  \n   Choose the \\(t\\) non\u2011sunny lines to be the diagonals\n   \\[\n   x+y=n+1,\\;x+y=n,\\;x+y=n-1,\\dots ,x+y=n+2-t .\n   \\]\n   These \\(t\\) diagonals cover all points of the \\(t\\) largest diagonals of the triangle.\n   The remaining points lie on the diagonals\n   \\[\n   D_2,D_3,\\dots ,D_{n+1-t},\n   \\]\n   whose sizes are \\(1,2,\\dots ,n-t\\).  Because \\(s=n-t\\), we have exactly as many sunny lines as the\n   size of the largest uncovered diagonal.  It is a standard elementary construction to colour the\n   points of a triangular array of size \\(n-t\\) with \\(s=n-t\\) colours so that each colour class\n   consists of at most one point on each diagonal; each colour class is then a straight line with a\n   slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) (hence sunny).  An explicit realisation is obtained by\n   taking, for \\(j=1,\\dots ,s\\),\n   \\[\n   L_j:\\; y=\\frac{j-1}{j}\\,x+1 .\n   \\]\n   As shown in part\u202f2, these \\(s\\) sunny lines meet every point of the remaining triangle.\n   Consequently the whole set \\(S_n\\) is covered by the \\(t\\) chosen diagonals together with the\n   \\(s\\) sunny lines, and exactly \\(k=s\\) of the \\(n\\) lines are sunny.\n\n   Hence for every \\(n\\ge5\\) and every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) a configuration exists.\n\n5. **Collecting the results.**  \n   Combining the constructions for the extreme values, the explicit families for the small\n   exceptional cases, and the impossibility proofs for the missing values, we obtain the complete\n   description stated in the Verdict.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\n#### 1.  Preliminaries  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(S_n\\) is the triangular number  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\).  \nConsequently a non\u2011sunny line is of one of the three forms  \n\n\\[\nx=c,\\qquad y=c,\\qquad x+y=c ,\\qquad c\\in\\mathbb R .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nFor a diagonal  \n\n\\[\nD_c:=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a+b=c\\},\\qquad 2\\le c\\le n+1,\n\\]\n\nwe have \\(|D_c|=c-1\\).  A line that is not of the form \\(x+y=c\\) meets each diagonal in at most one\npoint; a line of the form \\(x+y=c\\) meets that diagonal in all its points.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 2.  The extreme values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n\\)\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  The \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n  \\[\n  L_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n)\n  \\]\n\n  are all non\u2011sunny.  For any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); hence the point lies on the\n  line \\(x=a\\).  Thus the \\(n\\) lines cover \\(S_n\\) and exactly \\(k=0\\) of them are sunny.\n\n* **\\(k=n\\).**  \n  For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define  \n\n  \\[\n  L_i:\\;y=\\frac{i-1}{i}\\,x+1 .\n  \\tag{3}\n  \\]\n\n  The slope \\(\\frac{i-1}{i}\\) is never \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); therefore every \\(L_i\\) is sunny.\n  Let \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) and put \\(i:=a+b-1\\) (so \\(1\\le i\\le n\\)).  Substituting \\(i\\) into (3) gives  \n\n  \\[\n  y=\\frac{a+b-2}{a+b-1}\\,x+1 .\n  \\]\n\n  Taking \\(x=a\\) yields \\(y=b\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_{a+b-1}\\).\n  Hence the \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=1}^{n}\\) cover the whole set \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 3.  The cases \\(n=3\\) and \\(n=4\\)\n\n##### 3.1  The case \\(n=3\\)\n\n\\(S_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\\).\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  The three vertical lines \\(x=1,2,3\\) work.\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  Take the sunny line \\(y=x\\) together with the two non\u2011sunny lines\n  \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\).  Every point of \\(S_3\\) belongs to at least one of them, and only\n  \\(y=x\\) is sunny.\n* **\\(k=3\\).**  The three sunny lines  \n\n  \\[\n  y=x,\\qquad y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32,\\qquad y=-2x+5\n  \\]\n\n  cover all six points.\n* **\\(k=2\\) is impossible.**  \n  Suppose three distinct lines cover \\(S_3\\) and exactly two of them are sunny.\n  The unique non\u2011sunny line must be of one of the three forms in (2).  A direct\n  inspection of the three possibilities shows that in each case at least two points of\n  \\(S_3\\) remain uncovered, contradicting the hypothesis.  Hence \\(k=2\\) cannot occur.\n\nThus for \\(n=3\\)\n\n\\[\n\\{k\\mid\\text{admissible}\\}=\\{0,1,3\\}.\n\\]\n\n##### 3.2  The case \\(n=4\\)\n\n\\(S_4\\) consists of the ten points  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4),\\\\\n&(2,1),(2,2),(2,3),\\\\\n&(3,1),(3,2),\\\\\n&(4,1).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  The four vertical lines \\(x=1,2,3,4\\) work.\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  Use the sunny line \\(y=x\\) together with the three diagonal lines\n  \\(x+y=5,\\;x+y=4,\\;x+y=3\\).  Every point of \\(S_4\\) lies on at least one of these four\n  lines, and only \\(y=x\\) is sunny.\n* **\\(k=3\\).**  The three sunny lines  \n\n  \\[\n  L_1:\\;y=x,\\qquad\n  L_2:\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\\qquad\n  L_3:\\;y=-2x+7,\n  \\]\n\n  together with the non\u2011sunny diagonal line \\(x+y=5\\) cover all ten points.\n* **\\(k=4\\).**  The four sunny lines  \n\n  \\[\n  y=x,\\qquad y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\\qquad y=-2x+7,\\qquad\n  y=-\\tfrac13x+\\tfrac43\n  \\]\n\n  cover \\(S_4\\); a straightforward verification shows that each point belongs to at least\n  one of them.\n* **\\(k=2\\) is impossible.**  \n  The diagonal \\(D_5=\\{(1,4),(2,3),(3,2),(4,1)\\}\\) contains four points, therefore each of\n  the four lines must meet \\(D_5\\) in a distinct integer point.  If a line is non\u2011sunny,\n  by (2) it is either vertical, horizontal or the diagonal \\(x+y=5\\).  The diagonal\n  \\(x+y=5\\) meets only the points of \\(D_5\\) and none of the other diagonals; consequently\n  at most one of the four lines can be non\u2011sunny.  Hence at least three lines must be sunny,\n  contradicting the requirement that exactly two be sunny.  Therefore \\(k=2\\) cannot be\n  realised for \\(n=4\\).\n\nThus for \\(n=4\\)\n\n\\[\n\\{k\\mid\\text{admissible}\\}=\\{0,1,3,4\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 4.  The general case \\(n\\ge 5\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  Write  \n\n\\[\ns:=k\\qquad\\text{(number of sunny lines)},\\qquad\nt:=n-s\\qquad\\text{(number of non\u2011sunny lines)} .\n\\]\n\nChoose the \\(t\\) non\u2011sunny lines to be the diagonals  \n\n\\[\nx+y=n+1,\\;x+y=n,\\;x+y=n-1,\\dots ,x+y=n+2-t .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThese \\(t\\) diagonals cover all points belonging to the \\(t\\) largest diagonals of the\ntriangular set \\(S_n\\).  The points that are still uncovered lie on the diagonals  \n\n\\[\nD_2,D_3,\\dots ,D_{n+1-t},\n\\]\n\nwhose sizes are \\(1,2,\\dots ,n-t\\).  Because \\(s=n-t\\), the number of sunny lines equals the\nsize of the largest uncovered diagonal.\n\nNow apply the construction of Section\u202f2 with the parameter \\(n\\) replaced by \\(n-t\\):\nfor \\(j=1,\\dots ,s\\) put  \n\n\\[\nL_j:\\;y=\\frac{j-1}{j}\\,x+1 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nEach line \\(L_j\\) is sunny, and as proved in Section\u202f2 it meets every point of the triangle\n\\(\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge1,\\ a+b\\le (n-t)+1\\}\\); that triangle is precisely the set of points that\nremain after removing the diagonals (4).  Consequently the family consisting of the \\(t\\) diagonals\n(4) together with the \\(s\\) sunny lines (5) covers the whole set \\(S_n\\), and exactly \\(k=s\\) of\nthe \\(n\\) lines are sunny.\n\nThus for every \\(n\\ge5\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) a required configuration\nexists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 5.  Conclusion  \n\nCollecting the results obtained in Sections\u202f3 and\u202f4 we obtain the complete description of\nthe admissible numbers of sunny lines.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\nn & \\text{admissible }k\\\\ \\hline\n3 & \\{0,1,3\\}\\\\\n4 & \\{0,1,3,4\\}\\\\\nn\\ge5 & \\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\end{array}}\n\\]\n\nAll these values are attainable, and the missing values for \\(n=3\\) and \\(n=4\\) are proved to be\nimpossible.  Hence the problem is completely solved."
